= MSc in mathematical and computational finance course of the University of Oxford
{parent=Master's course of the University of Oxford}
{c}

https://www.ox.ac.uk/admissions/graduate/courses/msc-mathematical-and-computational-finance[Official course description].

Introductory week followed by core courses: stochastic <calculus>; financial derivatives; <numerical methods>; <statistics> and financial data analysis; financial computing with C++ in each of the first two terms; deep learning; quantitative risk management; <stochastic control>; fixed income. Four electives complete the taught programme. Third-term dissertation, potentially prepared in connection with an industry internship.

= MSc in mathematical and theoretical physics course of the University of Oxford
{parent=Master's course of the University of Oxford}
{c}

https://www.ox.ac.uk/admissions/graduate/courses/msc-mathematical-and-theoretical-physics[Official course description].

Ten assessment units in total, with free choice of lecture courses and an optional one- or two-unit dissertation. <Groups and representations course of the University of Oxford> is a shared examined subject. https://mmathphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/past-papers[Official joint-course paper archive]. Optional: either 30 pages for one unit or 60 pages for two units. This differs from the compulsory dissertations on the OMMS and MMSC.

= MSc in mathematical modelling and scientific computing course of the University of Oxford
{parent=Master's course of the University of Oxford}
{c}

https://www.ox.ac.uk/admissions/graduate/courses/msc-mathematical-modelling-and-scientific-computing[Official course description].

Required applied <partial differential equations> and perturbation methods; choose two or three numerical-analysis cores from numerical solution of <partial differential equations>, numerical <linear algebra> and continuous optimisation. At least two special-topic units, including modelling/methods and computation. One scientific-computing case study in Michaelmas term and one mathematical-modelling case study in Hilary term. Compulsory four-unit dissertation, normally written in Trinity term and the summer.

= MSc in mathematical sciences course of the University of Oxford
{parent=Master's course of the University of Oxford}
{c}

https://www.ox.ac.uk/admissions/graduate/courses/msc-mathematical-sciences[Official course description].

Six to eight taught units from the Part C <mathematics> and <statistics> menus. Shared branches include <Category theory course of the University of Oxford>, <Algebraic topology course of the University of Oxford>, <model theory> and <elliptic curves>. https://courses.maths.ox.ac.uk/[Current departmental catalogue]. Compulsory dissertation worth two units.

= MSc in mathematics and foundations of computer science course of the University of Oxford
{parent=Master's course of the University of Oxford}
{c}

https://www.ox.ac.uk/admissions/graduate/courses/msc-mathematics-and-foundations-of-computer-science[Official course description].

Basic-option schedule. The central description gives indicative choices including algebraic <topology>, <category theory>, <information theory> and quantum processes and computation; annual availability follows the handbook. https://courses.maths.ox.ac.uk/mod/book/tool/print/index.php?id=70514[2026–27 handbook]. Advanced-option schedule. Indicative choices include algebraic <geometry>, automata, logic and games, and axiomatic <set theory>. At least five lecture courses are required overall. Compulsory dissertation on a topic related to the taught material, undertaken in Trinity term and the summer.

